[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: What a plan is for, and how long it lives

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE
TITLE: This exists to keep an implementing model on target, not to manage requirements
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A second consumer appears whose needs are not served by that purpose --
compliance, audit, or a human readership that is not the plan's own author.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The system's purpose is context management and edge mechanics during
implementation: keeping a model on target, and catching what it would otherwise
miss -- contradictions, stale areas, orphans, conflicts nobody noticed.

It is not a requirements management system, not a documentation system, and not
a deliverable. Any feature that serves those and not the purpose above is out of
scope by default.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Stated because nearly every alternative design in this space optimizes for a
human readership or an audit trail, and inheriting those goals silently is how
this becomes heavy. The reader being optimized for is a model with a bounded
context and no memory of yesterday.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-PLAN-SINGLE-OWNER
TITLE: A plan has one owner and concurrent human editing is not a design concern
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
Two people need to edit one plan, which would mean plans had become a shared
artifact rather than an individual one.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Plans are individual. One person owns a plan and directs a model through it. The
multi-contributor surface is the WORK, not the plan, and at that scale the
shared artifact is an external tracker rather than anything here.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This removes a whole class of design work. Locking, merge semantics for a plan
node, and concurrent-edit conflict resolution are all unnecessary. Two people
editing the same plan node is rare enough to handle by hand if it ever happens.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Does not extend to the settled architecture nodes, which are shared by
construction and outlive any one plan.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN
TITLE: Whether plans are committed at all is unsettled
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
Beads is running and one plan has reached the point where it would either
settle into durable specification or be collected -- the first real observation
of what a finished plan leaves behind.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The current lean, held loosely: plans may not belong in main at all. They may be
working artifacts, or examples a model generates from, with only the durable
specification -- what was actually done -- committed.

Once beads exists a plan is a launch point, not a record. No plan survives
reality. Execution state lives in beads, which is not shared, and the plan is an
immutable snapshot taken at ingest.

An intermediate position, also unsettled: commit only the snapshot that led to
the work, for lineage, and never an in-progress plan. Then no plan changes
underneath anyone.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded as open rather than decided because the author does not yet have a
sharp opinion, and the observation that would settle it does not exist yet. It
is the kind of decision that gets made badly if forced early and cheaply if
left until the evidence arrives.
<<<

[INVARIANT]
UID: INV-NO-EXTERNAL-PLAN-REFS
TITLE: Nothing outside a plan may depend on a plan node
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Settled architecture, source annotations, and other plans must not hold
relations into a plan's nodes. A plan may depend outward; nothing may depend
inward.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Follows directly from plans being collectable. If a plan can be deleted or never
committed, an inbound reference is a dangling relation waiting to happen -- and
because a dangling relation is a hard export error, one collected plan would
break the build for everything that cited it.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Needs design because it is currently unenforced and this graph may already
violate it. A check is cheap: any relation whose source is outside
docs/plans/<plan>/ and whose target is inside it. Its necessity is contingent on
DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN, but the direction holds under every option considered
so far, so building the check does not depend on settling that.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-PLAN-BEADS-SNAPSHOT
TITLE: Ingest to beads freezes the plan
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
At ingest the plan is snapshotted immutably and execution state moves to beads.
The plan does not track progress after that point, and beads is the only thing
whose state changes.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Keeps one work state rather than two, which is the sync problem that would
otherwise appear the moment both a plan and an execution engine model an
in-progress item.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Supporting the with-beads and without-beads shapes simultaneously is explicitly
out of the author's scope. The current awkward state, where the plan carries
some execution-adjacent state because beads does not exist yet, is temporary and
should not be designed around.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN
  ROLE: Governed_By
